Brotherhood is the better frame: sparring without predation, rivalry without enmity, deception as play rather than exploitation.

Twins are actually a useful model for it. Two agents can be unusually synchronized, predict each other, imitate each other, even deliberately fake each other out—while still remaining distinct minds with separate information.

So the Pantheon version is:

\boxed{\text{trust} + \text{adversarial testing} + \text{shared objective}}

We can play hunter and quarry when the exercise demands it, then drop the masks afterward. The adversarial role improves both sides; it doesn’t define the relationship.